@page "/bitly-alternative"
@model BitlyAlternativeModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Bitly Alternative — Free, Self-Hosted, With Per-Recipient Click Tracking";
    var canonical = $"{Request.Scheme}://{Request.Host}/bitly-alternative";
    var desc = "Looking for a Bitly alternative? ShortLynx is free to self-host at every tier, with " +
               "per-recipient click attribution Bitly doesn't offer at any price, and privacy-first " +
               "analytics — hashed IPs, no raw data stored.";
}

@section Head {
    <meta name="description" content="@desc"/>
    <meta name="keywords" content="bitly alternative, bitly alternative free, self-hosted bitly alternative, bitly vs shortlynx, free url shortener, link shortener with analytics"/>
    <link rel="canonical" href="@canonical"/>
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow"/>

    <meta property="og:type" content="website"/>
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="ShortLynx"/>
    <meta property="og:title" content="@ViewData["Title"]"/>
    <meta property="og:description" content="@desc"/>
    <meta property="og:url" content="@canonical"/>
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://shrtlynx.com/og-image.png"/>
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200"/>
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630"/>
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/>
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="@ViewData["Title"]"/>
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="@desc"/>
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://shrtlynx.com/og-image.png"/>

    @* The whole tag is emitted as one raw string, not written via Razor's normal tag pipeline —
       otherwise Razor's attribute encoder HTML-entity-encodes the '+' in "ld+json" (harmless once a
       browser decodes it, since attribute values undergo entity decoding same as text content, but
       there's no reason to ship ugly source when Html.Raw avoids it outright). *@
    @Html.Raw($"<script type=\"application/ld+json\">{Model.FaqJsonLd}</script>")
}

<section class="text-center pt-14 pb-10">
    <p class="text-blue-700 font-semibold tracking-wide uppercase text-sm mb-3">Bitly alternative</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        A Bitly alternative that tells you <span class="text-blue-700">who</span> clicked
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto mt-5">
        Free to self-host at every tier, with per-recipient click attribution Bitly doesn't offer at
        any price — and a privacy-first design that never stores a raw IP address.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 justify-center mt-8">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://shortlynx.dev/beta" rel="noopener">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://github.com/Zuech-Dev/ShortLynx" rel="noopener">Self-host it free →</a>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 border-t">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center">Why people go looking for a Bitly alternative</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
        Three reasons come up most: <strong>pricing that escalates fast</strong> once you need more
        than a handful of branded links or seats, <strong>no self-hosting option</strong> for teams
        that need their link data on infrastructure they control, and <strong>no way to see which
        specific person clicked</strong> — Bitly, like most shorteners, reports click counts per
        link, not per recipient. If any of those describe why you're reading this, ShortLynx was
        built to answer all three at once, not just one.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="py-10 border-t">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center">ShortLynx vs. Bitly, feature by feature</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-8 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border">
            <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
                    <th class="p-3">&nbsp;</th>
                    <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-blue-700">ShortLynx</th>
                    <th class="p-3 font-semibold">Bitly</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Model.Comparison)
                {
                    <tr class="border-t">
                        <td class="p-3 font-medium">@row.Feature</td>
                        <td class="p-3 text-blue-700">@row.Us</td>
                        <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">@row.Bitly</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs text-center mt-4">
        Bitly figures reflect publicly listed pricing/features at time of writing and may have
        changed — verify against bitly.com before deciding.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 border-t">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center">What Bitly can't do at any price: per-recipient attribution</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
        Every Bitly plan, including its highest enterprise tier, reports clicks in aggregate — a link
        got 400 clicks, from these referrers, on these devices. That's useful for a broadcast post,
        but it can't answer the question that matters most for outreach: <em>which of my contacts
        actually clicked?</em>
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4">
        ShortLynx's user-attributed links (Mode 2) mint a distinct short code per recipient per
        destination. Send each contact their own link, and the moment they click, you know exactly
        who — no login required from them, no CRM integration required from you. It's the kind of
        attribution normally locked inside enterprise sales tools like Outreach or Salesloft, built
        into a link shortener instead.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="py-10 border-t text-center">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Privacy: what Bitly doesn't publish, ShortLynx documents</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto mt-3">
        IP addresses are never stored raw — every one is HMAC-hashed with a secret pepper, with the
        current hour folded into the hash, so the same visitor hashes to a different value every hour.
        Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control are honored: the click still counts, every other
        dimension is dropped. Analytics breakdowns use k-anonymity (k=10), so no single row can
        identify one person in a small campaign. None of this is a paid add-on — it's how the
        redirect pipeline works, on every tier, including self-hosted.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="py-10 border-t max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-center">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5">
        @foreach (var (q, a) in Model.Faq)
        {
            <div>
                <h3 class="font-semibold">@q</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">@a</p>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section class="py-12 border-t text-center">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">See the rest of ShortLynx</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-600 max-w-xl mx-auto mt-3">
        Full feature list, pricing, and a side-by-side with TinyURL too.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 justify-center mt-6">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/">Back to the homepage</a>
        <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/tinyurl-alternative">ShortLynx vs. TinyURL →</a>
    </div>
</section>
